From: FrontierAIAccountabilityProject
 <315748336+FrontierAIAccountabilityProject@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 13:00:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] queue: capture the sweep's drafted fixes as CUREs 8-16,
 marked sweep-derived

The sweep diagnosed; this files the treatment. Nine new cures, every ANCHOR
verified verbatim against the tagged statute and occurring exactly once, each
carrying an administrative-load line per the new process rule. All are marked
SWEEP-DERIVED, NOT MAINTAINER-DRAFTED and published as a hypothesis about the
fix rather than settled drafting -- because the lesson the sweep just
delivered is that same-week solo drafting produces fatal defects, and
answering it with seven more same-evening cures would repeat the error.

- CURE 8, SEC. 6: the individual-liability offense reconstructed. Restores
  the predicate-violation and nexus elements so it can be pleaded at all;
  adds the missing converse (a person who took every measure within her
  authority HAS exercised due care); makes due care an element rather than a
  sentencing gate, curing the Alleyne problem; restores Park's production
  burden, which is the difference between a statute and a monument; and
  conforms SEC. 6(e) to SEC. 4(a) so 'practical power' stops having two
  contents in one Act.
- CURE 9, SEC. 10(e): the access-and-demand authority. SEC. 5(e) punishes
  refusing a demand no provision authorises -- the Act imported 21 USC 331(e)
  and left section 374 behind. Ranked into SEC. 13(b)(1) first rank, since an
  offense whose enabling authority is unranked is the defect SEC. 13(b)(5)
  exists to prevent.
- CURE 10, SEC. 3(c)(3): four interim controls so SEC. 5(b) is not dormant
  until year four. Each is a control the 2026 record names as absent BY NAME
  -- which is also the fair-notice answer.
- CURE 11, SEC. 5 and 9(b): name the obligor. No person is currently
  commanded to report anywhere in the Act.
- CURE 12, SEC. 5(d): restore scienter. The note cites 18 USC 1001, which
  requires knowingly and willfully; the text has neither, making it a
  strict-liability false-statement crime in the first severability rank.
- CURE 13, SEC. 1(b)(1)(B): say sever, not extend. The undefined phrase
  currently decides whether an academic fine-tune carries the full developer
  stack.
- CURE 14, SEC. 9(b) and new 9(d): a detection clock that cannot be gamed by
  certifying less monitoring, and the duty to notify the people whose systems
  were breached -- which the Act, extraordinarily, does not currently contain.
- CURE 15, SEC. 3(c)(2)(E): a disclose-and-cure valve, because the text as
  drafted makes the honest filer the easier defendant.
- CURE 16, SEC. 1(b)(7): a deception limb preserving Van Buren, because Van
  Buren excludes the AISI conduct -- fabricated identities and sockpuppet
  endorsement are gates-UP actions.

And the conclusion the queue now states in its own hold notice: with defects
reaching SEC. 6, SEC. 10, SEC. 5(b)'s commencement and the Act's
jurisdictional reach, v3.5 is a REBUILD rather than a splice. That decision is
owed before assembly begins.

Tagged v3.4 text unchanged (E10).
